  "answer_text": "Climate change is having wide-ranging and deeply serious effects on ocean life. Here is a comprehensive overview of the key impacts:\n\n---\n\n### \ud83c\udf21\ufe0f 1. Rising Ocean Temperatures & Marine Heatwaves\n\nLife in most of the global ocean, from pole to pole and from sea surface to the abyssal depths, is already experiencing higher temperatures due to human-driven climate change. Marine heatwaves take a particularly severe toll \u2014 for example, due to the 2019\u20132021 Pacific Northwest marine heatwave, Bering Sea snow crab populations declined 84% between 2018 and 2022, a loss of 9.8 billion crabs. Scientists predict that the frequency, duration, scale, and intensity of marine heatwaves will increase as sea surface temperatures continue to rise with global warming.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83e\uddea 2. Ocean Acidification\n\nIncreased carbon dioxide in the air makes oceans more acidic. This occurs when seawater absorbs carbon dioxide and mixes it with water to form carbonic acid, decreasing ocean pH. The acid in the water can badly affect sea creatures that need calcium carbonate to make their shells and bones. These creatures also act as carbon stores, helping to mitigate climate change, but as their shells dissolve, this natural way of removing carbon from the atmosphere is threatened.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udc20 3. Coral Reef Destruction\n\nDubbed the \"rainforests of the sea,\" coral reefs are home to more than 25% of marine life in the world, including over 4,000 fish species, turtles, plants, invertebrates, and marine mammals. Warming ocean temperatures can lead to coral bleaching \u2014 the sudden die-off of large parts of coral reefs. A warming of 1.5\u00b0C threatens to destroy 70 to 90 percent of coral reefs, and a 2\u00b0C increase means a nearly 100 percent loss \u2014 a point of no return.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83c\udf0a 4. Ocean Deoxygenation\n\nOverall ocean oxygen concentrations are estimated to have declined 2% over 50 years from the 1960s. Low oxygen represents a stress for almost all marine animals, and very low oxygen levels create regions with much reduced fauna. It is predicted that these low oxygen zones will expand in future due to climate change, representing a serious threat to marine life.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udc1f 5. Shifting Species Distribution & Biodiversity Loss\n\nWidespread changes have been observed, including damage to coral reefs and mangroves that support ocean life, and migration of species to higher latitudes and altitudes where the water could be cooler. These changes have resulted in habitat loss, population declines, increased risks of species extirpations and extinctions, and rearrangements of marine food webs. Latest estimates from UNESCO warn that more than half of the world's marine species may stand on the brink of extinction by 2100.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83c\udf3f 6. Impact on Phytoplankton & the Food Chain\n\nOne of the most critical changes brought about by global warming is the ongoing reduction of phytoplankton. These tiny plants are an integral food source for ocean life and are responsible for around half of the world's photosynthetic activity \u2014 they are essentially the foundation of the oceanic food chain, so a reduction in their numbers creates a knock-on effect that ripples up the entire food chain.\n\n---\n\n### \u26a0\ufe0f 7. The \"Deadly Trio\"\n\nThe stressors of increased global warming, ocean acidification, and increased deoxygenation \u2014 together commonly referred to as climate change's \"deadly trio\" \u2014 when occurring at the same time, cause synergistic effects that damage marine life and ecosystem structure and function. A study published in 2025 projected that rising ocean temperatures, together with other climate-driven stressors, will more than double cumulative impacts on marine ecosystems by mid-century.\n\n---\n\nThe effects of climate change on ocean life are interconnected and cascading. Protecting our oceans requires urgent action to reduce greenhouse gas emissions and limit global warming to the lowest possible levels.",
